                                       "kibana.version":  "9.4.2",
                                       "kibana.alert.rule.parameters":  {
                                                                            "description":  "Detects PowerShell building a command or cmdlet name from an array of character\ncodes and rejoining it before execution, for example\n[char[]](112,111,119,101,114,115,104,101,108,108) piped through -join.\n\nThis rule exists because a real Atomic Red Team variation evaded the whole rule\npack. T1027 test #11 obfuscates Start-Process by constructing both the interpreter\nname and the command from decimal [char[]] arrays, then invoking with \u0026 (-join ...).\nIt carries no -EncodedCommand, no download-cradle vocabulary, and no scriptlet, so\nthe encoded-command rule (1131fb39), the decoded-cradle rule (8c1d2f4a), and the\nLOLBin rules all miss it. Its Sysmon Event ID 1 command line was captured intact in\nthe lab (2026-08-02 atomic run) and every deployed rule returned zero hits against it.\n\nThe discriminator is the obfuscation idiom itself: a [char[]] cast paired with -join\nis how a decimal code array is turned back into runnable text. Legitimate automation\nessentially never assembles a command name this way, so the two markers are required\ntogether to keep the rule tight.\n",
                                                                            "risk_score":  73,
                                                                            "severity":  "high",
                                                                            "license":  "DRL",
